    code: { lang: "python", title: "log_parser.py — failed logins per IP (complete, runnable)", code: "import sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef parse_failed_logins(path):\n    \"\"\"Return a Counter of source IPs from 'Failed password' lines.\"\"\"\n    counts = Counter()\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as f:\n        for line in f:\n            if 'Failed password' not in line:\n                continue\n            parts = line.split()\n            if 'from' in parts:\n                ip = parts[parts.index('from') + 1]\n                counts[ip] += 1\n    return counts\n\ndef main():\n    path = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 'auth.log'\n    try:\n        counts = parse_failed_logins(path)\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print(f'Error: file not found: {path}')\n        return 1\n    for ip, total in counts.most_common(10):\n        print(f'{total:>5}  {ip}')\n    return 0\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    raise SystemExit(main())", output: "   41  203.0.113.44\n   17  198.51.100.23\n(illustrative — matches the bash pipeline's result on the same simulated log)", lines: ["Counter is the standard library's counting dict — most_common() ranks for free.", "The 'with' block closes the file even if an error occurs mid-read.", "errors='replace' keeps one corrupt byte from killing a million-line parse.", "Finding 'from' then taking the next token mirrors the awk approach — robust to varying prefixes.", "FileNotFoundError becomes a clean message and exit code 1, not a traceback.", "The __main__ guard lets other scripts import parse_failed_logins without running main."] },
