      "JavaScript runs in the page, manipulating the Document Object Model (the DOM — the live tree of elements). Events (click, submit, input) trigger functions; fetch() makes HTTP requests and returns Promises, handled with async/await. Variables declared with const/let are block-scoped; the era of var-everywhere is legacy code you will still meet in audits.",
      "The browser's core security boundary is the same-origin policy: a script from origin A (scheme + host + port) cannot read responses or DOM from origin B unless B permits it (CORS). Cookies ride along automatically on requests to their domain — which is exactly why CSRF works and why SameSite cookies matter (Level 5/8). localStorage and sessionStorage hold data any script on the origin can read, so tokens stored there are one XSS away from theft — a trade-off you will evaluate as a professional.",
      "Reading code defensively: find where user input enters (location.search, form values, postMessage), where it lands (innerHTML is the dangerous sink — it parses HTML; textContent is the safe one), and which network calls leave with credentials. That input→sink habit is the seed of code auditing in Level 21.",
