    id: "l5-http-sessions", level: 5, title: "HTTP, Cookies, and Sessions — the Web's Memory",
    difficulty: "Foundation", minutes: 40, xp: 80, skill: "Web Security",
    prereq: "Level 4 complete.",
    objectives: ["Read a raw HTTP request/response pair fluently", "Explain methods, status codes, and the headers that matter", "Describe how cookies create sessions on a stateless protocol", "Set and judge cookie flags: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite"],
    relevance: "Web security is HTTP security. Burp Suite, ZAP, curl output, and every OWASP exercise speak this protocol; session handling flaws remain a top cause of account takeover.",
    concept: [
      "HTTP is a text protocol of requests and responses. A request names a method (GET reads, POST submits/creates, PUT/PATCH update, DELETE removes), a path, headers, and optionally a body. The response answers with a status code family (2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx your fault, 5xx server's fault) plus its own headers and body. APIs speak the same language with JSON bodies — Level 17 builds directly on this.",
      "HTTP is stateless: each request forgets the last. Sessions fake memory — the server issues an unguessable session ID, the browser stores it in a cookie, and the browser returns it automatically on every request to that domain. Whoever presents the ID is treated as that user, which is why session IDs are credentials: they must be long, random, rotated after login, and never placed in URLs.",
      "Cookie flags are the session's armor. Secure: only over HTTPS. HttpOnly: JavaScript cannot read it (blunts XSS theft). SameSite=Lax/Strict: the browser withholds the cookie on most cross-site requests (blunts CSRF). Missing armor is a graded finding — the 'Security Headers' lab and CTF 'ctf-headers' train exactly this review, and authentication (পরিচয় যাচাই) failures sit at A07 in OWASP Top 10:2025.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "One login, four messages",
    diagram: ["1. POST /login  (form: user + password)          → browser → server", "2. 200 OK  Set-Cookie: sid=9f2…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax", "3. GET /account  Cookie: sid=9f2…                 → 'you are logged in'", "4. POST /logout → server invalidates sid server-side; cookie expires"],
    terms: [
      { term: "Stateless", def: "The protocol keeps no memory between requests; sessions are an application-layer construction." },
      { term: "Session ID", def: "An unguessable token standing in for 'this authenticated user' — treat it as a password." },
      { term: "Set-Cookie", def: "The response header that plants/updates a cookie, with its flags." },
      { term: "Idempotent", def: "Safe to repeat with the same result (GET, PUT, DELETE) — POST is not." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "bash", title: "Read a real response's headers (example.com — IANA's test domain)", code: "curl -I https://example.com\n# -I asks for headers only. Look for the teaching points:\n#   HTTP/2 200            -> status line\n#   content-type          -> what the body is\n#   strict-transport-security / content-security-policy may be present\n#\n# Now watch a full conversation with a site YOU run locally:\ncurl -v http://localhost:8080/ 2>&1 | head -30", output: "HTTP/2 200 and a header block for example.com (exact headers vary — that variance is the lesson).", lines: ["curl -I is the fastest header audit there is — the Security Headers lab starts here.", "Status first, headers second, body last: read responses in that order.", "-v shows the request you sent too — compare both directions in Burp later.", "localhost testing needs no one's permission; third-party scanning does."] },
    mistakes: ["Putting session IDs in URLs (they leak via logs, history, and Referer headers).", "Setting cookies without flags 'because the framework default looked fine'.", "Treating a 200 OK as 'secure' — status says nothing about protection."],
    security: "Session security checklist you will reuse professionally: random IDs ≥128 bits of entropy, rotation on login/privilege change, idle + absolute timeouts, server-side invalidation at logout, and the three cookie flags by default.",
