    objectives: ["Order the IR lifecycle and explain order of volatility", "Preserve evidence with hashing and a chain-of-custody record", "Build a timeline from Windows events and Linux logs", "Decide containment that helps rather than harms"],
    relevance: "When an incident lands, the first hour decides whether the investigation succeeds. These are the habits — preserve first, document everything, contain deliberately — drilled in SOC Training and assessed in Project C.",
    concept: [
      "The lifecycle: Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned (NIST SP 800-61). Preparation is invisible until it saves you: logging on, backups tested, contacts listed, authority to act agreed. In the first hour your jobs are to confirm something real is happening, preserve evidence before it evaporates, and start a timeline — every entry with a timestamp and a source.",
      "Order of volatility governs collection: registers and cache die first, then RAM (running processes, network connections, decrypted content), then disk, then backups and logs held elsewhere. Powering off a machine 'to stop the attack' destroys RAM evidence and can trigger disk encryption lockouts — containment usually means network isolation, not the power button, unless destruction is actively ongoing. Imaging uses write-blockers and hashing (your Level 6 skill) so the copy can be proven identical to the original; the chain-of-custody form records who held the evidence, when, and why.",
      "Timelines turn chaos into a story: logon events (4624/4625), process starts, file writes, DNS queries, firewall denials — normalized to one clock (UTC in reports) and one table. The analyst's notebook is evidence too: contemporaneous, factual, free of speculation — mark analysis as analysis, exactly as CyberLab's intelligence policy requires.",
    ],
    diagramTitle: "First-hour checklist",
    diagram: ["1. Confirm & declare (who is incident lead?)", "2. Preserve: snapshot/VM state, RAM if trained & equipped, logs exported", "3. Isolate the host from the network (don't power off by default)", "4. Start the timeline + custody record; hash everything you copy", "5. Scope: what else did this account/host touch? Then eradicate & recover"],
    terms: [
      { term: "Chain of custody", def: "The signed record of evidence possession from collection to courtroom/report." },
      { term: "Order of volatility", def: "Collect fastest-disappearing evidence first: memory before disk." },
      { term: "Write blocker", def: "Hardware/software that prevents any writes to source media during imaging." },
      { term: "Containment", def: "Limiting the incident's reach (isolation, account disable) without destroying evidence." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "bash", title: "Preserve a log and prove it unchanged (your lab machine)", code: "# Copy first, analyze the copy. Hash both; the hashes must match.\ncp /var/log/auth.log ~/evidence/auth-2026-11-12.log\nsha256sum /var/log/auth.log ~/evidence/auth-2026-11-12.log\n# Record in your notes: source host, UTC time, who collected, why.\n# Windows equivalent: Copy-Item + Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256", output: "Two identical SHA-256 lines = the copy is bit-for-bit the original at collection time.", lines: ["Analyze copies, preserve originals — the forensic prime directive.", "The hash is only meaningful with the custody note beside it.", "UTC in all incident records; convert local times in parentheses if needed.", "Simulated incidents in SOC Training grade exactly these steps."] },
    mistakes: ["Rebooting an infected host as a first reflex — RAM evidence and volatile connections die with it.", "Running cleanup tools before imaging (you become the second incident).", "Writing opinions ('the attacker was…') in the factual timeline instead of in a labelled analysis section."],
    security: "Legal note: forensic work on systems you don't own requires the same written authorization as testing — and evidence handling may have legal requirements; escalate to qualified responders for real incidents beyond your scope.",
    pc: ["Practise the copy+hash ritual on any log; create a custody-note template in Research Workspace.", "Export your own Windows Security log (wevtutil) and build a 10-row timeline of your own logons."],
    android: ["Phones are evidence too: airplane mode preserves state better than power-off for a first response — and real device forensics belongs to qualified practitioners."],
    lab: "Guided lab: 'Timeline Reconstruction' from simulated event sets.",
    mission: "Mission: Project C rehearsal — a packet/log bundle (simulated) becomes an incident report.",
    practical: "Practical assessment: order six evidence sources by volatility and justify the order.",
    quiz: [
      { q: "Why isolate rather than power off a compromised host?", options: ["Isolation is faster paperwork", "Power-off destroys volatile evidence (RAM, connections) and may lock encrypted disks; isolation preserves both evidence and safety", "Powered-off hosts can't be hacked", "Policy says so, no reason"], answer: 1, why: "Containment should preserve the option to investigate — volatility order decides." },
      { q: "A chain-of-custody record exists to:", options: ["Encrypt evidence", "Prove who held evidence and that it was not altered in transit/storage", "Compress images", "Assign blame"], answer: 1, why: "With hashes, custody makes evidence trustworthy and admissible-quality." },
      { q: "Timestamps in incident reports should be recorded in:", options: ["Local time only", "UTC (with local conversions noted)", "Whatever the log used, mixed freely", "Relative terms like 'later'"], answer: 1, why: "One clock, or your timeline cannot correlate across systems and time zones." },
    ],
    summary: "Preserve first, isolate deliberately, hash and document everything, build the timeline. Calm procedure beats heroic improvisation.",
    skillGained: "Incident response and forensic discipline.",
    version: V, reviewed: REV, reference: "NIST SP 800-61r2 (Computer Security Incident Handling Guide).",
    limitations: TESTED,
